# AREA 51 configuration — template. Copy to `.env` (gitignored) and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   ./a51 setup
#
# `.env` is the single source of truth for every deploy-time value. `./a51 setup`
# writes back into this file as it discovers or provisions things (account id,
# database id, hostnames, generated secret), so after a successful setup your
# `.env` describes the whole deployment. Everything below is documented in
# docs/reference/configuration.md.
#
# Only ONE value is truly required to start: CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN. Setup prompts
# for anything else it needs and fills in the rest.

# ─── Cloudflare credentials ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

# API token. Create at: Cloudflare dashboard → My Profile → API Tokens →
# Create Token → Custom token. Fourteen permissions (see docs/guides/getting-started.md#api-token):
#   Account · Workers Scripts:Edit          Account · Workers R2 Storage:Edit
#   Account · D1:Edit                       Account · Cloudflare Pages:Edit
#   Account · Account Settings:Read         Account · Access: Apps and Policies:Edit
#   Account · Access: Organizations, Identity Providers, and Groups:Edit
#   Account · Email Routing Addresses:Edit
#   Zone    · Zone:Read                     Zone · DNS:Edit
#   Zone    · Zone Settings:Edit            Zone · Workers Routes:Edit
#   Zone    · Email Routing Rules:Edit
# Two traps that both look like "permission is set but still denied":
#   1. Zone · Zone Settings:Edit is what ENABLES Email Routing (it writes the
#      MX/SPF records). Email Routing Rules only covers the catch-all rule.
#      Missing Zone Settings => [10000] Authentication error on the enable step.
#   2. Under "Zone Resources", Include the zone you deploy onto (or All zones) —
#      a zone permission scoped elsewhere fails the same way as a missing one.
# A freshly created/edited token can also take ~a minute to propagate; setup
# retries auth errors a few times, but a long propagation just needs a re-run.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# Account ID. Left blank, setup discovers it (and asks if the token can see
# more than one account), then writes it back here.
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=

# ─── Hostnames ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The zone (a domain already added to this Cloudflare account) that setup
# provisions on. This is the ONLY layout value setup asks for: leave it blank and
# it lists your zones and prompts. Everything below is derived from the answer.
CLOUDFLARE_ZONE=

# AREA 51 dashboard (Cloudflare Pages + Cloudflare Access). Humans only.
# Blank = derived as area51.<zone>. Set it to override, including onto another
# zone; setup uses an existing value as-is and never asks.
DASHBOARD_HOSTNAME=

# Autopilot — the MCP / REST server AI agents connect to. Secret-authenticated.
# Blank = derived as autopilot.<zone>. Same override rule as above.
AUTOPILOT_HOSTNAME=

# Black hole — the catch-all host targets are pointed at. Public by necessity.
#
# DERIVED, NOT CONFIGURED: `./a51 setup` always sets this to CLOUDFLARE_ZONE and
# overwrites whatever is here. The catch-all rule that delivers mail to the
# catcher is zone-scoped and covers the apex PLUS every subdomain enabled for
# Email Routing — but nothing on the zone can capture mail until the apex is a
# mail black hole, because that is what puts the catch-all there. Setup
# establishes that foundation once.
# Extra black holes, mail on a subdomain included, go through
# `./a51 black-holes add <host> [http,mail]`.
BLACK_HOLE_HOSTNAME=

# Roles for the black hole above. Also derived: always http,mail. Per-host roles
# still apply to additional black holes: `./a51 black-holes add <host> http`.
BLACK_HOLE_ROLES=http,mail

# ─── Operators (dashboard access + Autopilot keys) ───────────────────────────
# WHO may use this deployment is NOT configured here. The source of truth is the
# `users` table in D1, managed with `./a51 users add|remove|list|rotate-secret`,
# so a change takes effect live with no redeploy — and so there is never a second
# copy of the list to drift out of step.
#
# One person, two independent doors:
#   · The dashboard is guarded by Cloudflare Access. No key is involved —
#     Cloudflare emails a one-time PIN to the address. Setup creates a Zero Trust
#     organization (if the account has none), enables One-time PIN login, and
#     puts an Access application in front of DASHBOARD_HOSTNAME.
#   · Autopilot is guarded by a per-operator API key, sent as
#     `Authorization: Bearer <key_id>_<secret>`. Only a hash is stored, so a key
#     is shown once when minted and replaced — never recovered — if lost.
#
# Neither the operators nor their keys appear in this file. They live in the D1
# `users` table, managed with `./a51 users`, and a key is shown exactly once when
# it is minted — including your own. That is not an oversight: a copy here would
# be a second answer to "who may log in", and a key at rest for no reason.
#
# Setup asks for the first operator's address when the table is empty, and stays
# quiet on every run after that. If a key is lost, replace it:
#   ./a51 users rotate-secret <email>
#
# The one thing you must NOT use as an operator address is anything on the
# black-hole domain — that domain is the trap, not your mailbox. The same real
# inbox can serve as an operator address and as FALLBACK_ADDRESS further down.

# Zero Trust team name — the <team>.cloudflareaccess.com subdomain used for the
# login screen. Only used when the account has no Zero Trust organization yet.
# Blank = derived from the zone name.
ACCESS_TEAM_NAME=

# How long an Access session lasts before re-authentication. Format: 30m, 24h, 730h.
ACCESS_SESSION_DURATION=24h

# The Zero Trust email list the Access policy points at. Filled in by setup.
#
# This is a Cloudflare resource id, not a copy of the allow-list: the operators
# themselves live in the D1 `users` table, and this list is a projection of the
# email column that `./a51 users` replaces wholesale on every add and remove.
# Editing that list by hand in the Cloudflare dashboard is pointless — the next
# `./a51 users` command overwrites it with whatever D1 says.
#
# Managing it needs `Account · Zero Trust:Edit` on the API token. Lists live
# under the Gateway resource tree, so `Access: Apps and Policies:Edit` does NOT
# cover them; that split is the most common cause of "the Access app updated but
# the list did not".
ACCESS_LIST_ID=

# ─── Storage ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# D1 database, shared by all three workers and the dashboard's Pages Functions.
D1_DATABASE_NAME=area51
# Filled in by setup after the database is created.
D1_DATABASE_ID=

# R2 bucket for captured email. One verbatim .eml per captured message at
# emails/<id>.eml. Bound as EML.
R2_BUCKET_NAME=area51-emails

# R2 bucket for endpoint file uploads — one object per file-backed endpoint,
# keyed by a random UUID. Separate from the email bucket so uploads can be
# wiped or given their own lifecycle rules independently. Bound as FILES.
R2_FILES_BUCKET_NAME=area51-files

# ─── Worker + project names ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cloudflare service names. Changing one after deploy creates a NEW worker and
# orphans the old one (along with its bound domains) — see docs/guides/operations.md.
WORKER_NAME=area51-black-holes
AGENT_WORKER_NAME=area51-autopilot
CLEANUP_WORKER_NAME=area51-cleanup
PAGES_PROJECT_NAME=area51

# ─── Black Holes worker ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Last-resort inbox. Used ONLY when email capture fails (R2 write throws, the
# handler errors); the original message is forwarded here instead of being lost.
# Successfully captured mail is never forwarded. Must be a verified Email
# Routing destination address on the account, or forwarding silently fails.
FALLBACK_ADDRESS=

# ─── Autopilot worker ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing to configure. Autopilot authenticates every call against the `users`
# table in D1 — there is no shared secret and no Worker Secret. Manage who can
# reach it with `./a51 users`.

# ─── Cleanup worker (retention) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Number of most-recent `requests` rows to keep; older rows are deleted.
CLEANUP_REQUESTS_KEEP=1000
# Max age in days for captured emails. Rows AND their R2 .eml objects older
# than this are deleted. Starred emails are exempt and kept forever.
CLEANUP_EMAIL_MAX_AGE_DAYS=90
# Cron schedule (UTC), standard 5 fields: min hour dom mon dow. Must be quoted.
CLEANUP_CRON="0 6 * * *"
